  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Friday)\nIssued at 1123 AM CST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nSaturday looks to be another pleasant day areawide. The surface\nridge will slowly shift eastward, bringing easterly to\nsoutheasterly winds to the region. Wind gusts could pick up later\nin the day, especially across eastern Oklahoma as a surface low \ntracks eastward into the Southern Plains by Saturday night. At the\nsame time, a mid level low will gradually track eastward through \nOklahoma Saturday night into Sunday, while weakening and becoming\ncutoff from the main jet stream spreading across the northern \nstates. Low level moisture will slowly return to eastern Oklahoma \nahead of this system, and widespread showers are expected to \nspread into eastern Oklahoma from the west beginning early Sunday \nmorning. Moderate to occasionally heavy showers look to last \nthrough much of the day Sunday over eastern Oklahoma, while drier \nair further west will limit the rain potential across far eastern \nOklahoma and western Arkansas. The heaviest rainfall is forecast \nacross the southern half of the CWA where up to an inch plus will \nbe possible by Sunday evening as things begin to wind down. \nLighter precipitation and totals are expected the further north \nand east you go across the region with only upward of a tenth of \nan inch across far northeast OK and northwest AR. Instability will\nbe very limited, so any thunder potential is very low, but some \nisolated thunder potential will be possible near the Red River \nduring the day Sunday. The widespread thick cloud cover and \nwidespread precip will keep temperatures near steady, generally in\nthe mid 40s Sunday across eastern Oklahoma. Highs could climb \ninto the 50s further east across Arkansas where thicker cloud \ncover and precipitation will be later to arrive.\n\nAny precipitation will mostly exit the area by Monday morning,\nthough lingering cloud cover through much of the day Monday will\nkeep conditions below normal for much of the area. Mid level\nridging will build in through the middle part of the week, with \nsoutherly surface flow returning. Thus, a warming trend will \ncommence with temperatures rising back above normal and quiet \nconditions persisting through at least Wednesday. Guidance \ndiverges on the pattern evolution through late week, but a more \nactive weather pattern is looking more likely late week into next \nweekend, though timing and location of the various features \nremains in low confidence at this point.\n\nBowlan",
